  "slug": "brain-mapping-9cc9",
  "summary": "Pretrained and geometric deep learning models now register, average, and classify structural data across cryo-EM, SMLM, and AFM modalities.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational recovery of structural information from sparse, noisy, or conformationally heterogeneous imaging data. Cryo-IEF, pre-trained on about 65 million cryo-EM particle images, supports classification, pose clustering, and quality assessment. The SMLM study applies a geometric deep learning autoencoder to perform template-free 3D particle averaging of sparse point clouds through latent-space averaging. The AFM study uses unsupervised deep learning to register and cluster image stacks by conformation, extending localization AFM to flexible membrane proteins. NanoporeDB differs in kind: it is a structural resource rather than an image-analysis method, providing over 6,600 multimeric protein nanopore models generated with AlphaFold-Multimer and AlphaFold3. What appears new is the shift toward template-free, latent-space approaches for sparse or conformationally variable targets, alongside large pretrained models applied to particle-level data.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-12-03"
}
